# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXES. Every unknown path on this site currently answers HTTP 200
# with the homepage, byte for byte, because the Pages project serves a
# single-page-app fallback. Verified against the live site: an invented path
# returns 200 and 29,083 bytes identical to /. So do the three pages deleted in
# PR #7, which therefore never signalled their removal to a search engine, and
# which _data/indexnow.json still re-submits to Bing on every deploy.
#
# The three rules below stop those three specific URLs resolving. They cannot
# shadow anything: no asset is published at any of these paths.
/joe-prich/                             /404.html  404
/rob-hein/                              /404.html  404
/prichards-air-conditioning-neo-nazi/   /404.html  404

# THE CATCH-ALL IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
#
# The general fix for the soft-404 behaviour is a final `/*  /404.html  404`
# rule. It is not here, and must not be added without being tested on a preview
# deployment first, because if Cloudflare Pages evaluates redirect rules BEFORE
# static assets rather than after, a catch-all takes the entire court record
# offline. That is not a risk worth reasoning about from documentation on a live
# litigation site.
#
# I could not run that test: this seat holds no Cloudflare credentials, so it
# can neither deploy a preview nor read the Pages project configuration to see
# whether the fallback is a project setting. Escalated rather than guessed.
#
# Whoever enables it: deploy to a preview branch, confirm that a known-good path
# still returns its own content and 200, and only then confirm that an unknown
# path returns 404. Checking the unknown path alone proves nothing, because a
# rule that breaks every page also makes unknown paths 404.
